WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.290
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.880
So I think one of the great unanswered questions

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.540
of world history is, "why cities?".

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.280
Like, for 95 percent of human history basically everyone was a hunter-gatherer

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.260
Living in a small band of maybe a few dozen people.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.320
And then around 12,000 years ago communities started to get bigger with the

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.080
advent of agriculture and domesticated livestock.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.620
Suddenly, one person could harvest and/or slaughter more than one person's

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.140
food needs, meaning that not everyone had to be in the food acquisition business.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.080
And that allowed for specialization of labor; professional priests, and scribes, and soldiers, and so on.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.500
But how and why that happened is the subject of much debate among historians,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:39.720
not least because the shift from hunting and gathering to agriculture seems to have been

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.160
completely irrational.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.880
For starters, hunter-gatherers seemed to have been healthier than early agriculturalists;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.800
like, analysis of human remains and archaeological sites show us that famine and

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.960
starvation were much more common among early settlement dwellers.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.920
Also, hunter-gatherers were less likely to die of disease pandemics because, you know,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.960
cities meant lots of people living together, and getting each other sick, and also living with livestock

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.140
so diseases could cross the species barrier.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.780
So, living in early agricultural settlements was bad for your health.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.380
It also probably meant more work. Like, studies of 20th century hunting and gathering communities showed that

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.180
members of those communities work on average less than 20 hours per week acquiring food.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.260
And also less than 20 hours per week on so-called "domestic labor",

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.880
which is everything from preparing food, to tool and shelter maintenance.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.840
Everything we can gather about early agricultural life is that it was a lot more labor-intensive,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.140
and certainly contemporary life is a lot more labor-intensive.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.760
Like Americans for instance work on average 40 hours a week, and spend another

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.980
36 hours per week on domestic labour.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.640
So why would anyone want to live in a city where your life is harder, but also shorter?

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.300
Well, there are many theories about this. Some people think the chicken of state power came first;

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.060
that like the moment there was a surplus it was taxed by the state and then the power structure became

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.340
inescapable for the people living under it.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.680
Others think the egg came first; that people chose to use the surplus to create a state that

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.580
could protect them from outside attack.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:01.040
Some theorized that then, as now, cities were just more exciting places to live with more connections to make.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.280
Others argue the relative equality of hunter-gatherer communities was unattractive to those who

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.660
wanted power, and that the drive to concentrate power drove the growth of cities.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.100
But whatever happened, it does seem like cities were not in these short or even medium term interest of those who

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:15.920
lived in them.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.360
Now, it'd be easy to say and, many have, that this was all a mistake, and we should have just remained

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:27.360
hunter-gatherers. Certainly agriculture, and the ensuing human-centric planet has been very bad for biodiversity on earth.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.860
But we also shouldn't romanticize pre-
agricultural communities where infanticide was usually

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.380
common and rates of death by violence were usually much higher than they are now.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.020
And while settlements do seem to have been initially disastrous for those who lived in them,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:44.420
they did eventually lead to better lives for humans by almost any measure.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.660
We are less likely to die of infectious disease, or malnutrition, or violence than ever,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.420
and while as I mentioned earlier, we do work more than hunter-gatherers,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.700
we also work less than we have at any point in the last 150 years.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:00.100
Meanwhile, absolute poverty is declining dramatically and median US household income is at its

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:01.080
highest point ever.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:05.940
But I'd actually argue the greatest benefit of cities has been reading and writing which have allowed us to

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:10.380
speed our accumulation of knowledge, and fuel discoveries in science and technology, and which could

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.380
only be invented in big communities with specialization of labor.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:18.660
But there were trade-offs to the choices our ancestors made, and there are trade-offs to the choices

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.880
we're making today.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.300
To live intentionally I think you have to bear in mind what you believe to be the ground of meaning in life.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:29.400
Like if the point of life is to preserve biodiversity on our home planet then hunting and gathering is the way to go.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.320
If the point is to seek an understanding of the universe and our place in it, then specialization of Labor is a

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.120
necessity.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.180
But I have to say that while I understand much has been lost, I am grateful to live in a world with

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:42.260
telescopes, and vaccines, and YouTube, and books.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.560
Late update! Speaking of books, Hank, I am so incredibly excited!

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.460
You are publishing a book next year! Fancy New York Times article in the doobly-doo below.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:53.140
I continue to be awed by your ability to do so many things so well.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:58.840
It is An Absolutely Remarkable Thing. That is the title but it's also how I feel. Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

